Return a peer stream's credit once its receive half is complete.

Applies to picoquic a602e60babea48da7849169d716aaf68684e66d6, after 0002,
0006, 0007 and 0008; it also applies without 0007.

picoquic replenishes MAX_STREAMS for a peer-initiated stream once its
receive half is complete. That timing is this provider's policy, which LibMoQ
relies on; RFC 9000 Section 4.6 grants cumulative permission and does not fix
when a receiver replenishes it. Upstream evaluates completion on the frame
that carries the FIN before storing that frame's final size, so it compares
the consumed offset with the old fin_offset -- the bytes delivered so far --
and counts a FIN that arrives past a gap prematurely, while bytes below the
final size (Section 4.5) are still missing. It also treats a reset as complete
only once the consumed offset reaches the reset's final size, which counts
bytes that will never arrive, so such a stream is deleted uncounted. The FIN
check now runs after the final size is stored, and a reset is complete once
its reliable prefix (zero for RESET_STREAM) is consumed. Duplicate terminals
are counted once (max_stream_updated).
Tests: adapters/picoquic/tests/test_pq_provider_stream_credit.c.

